// Cloudflare deploy config for mechcrate.dev.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY WORKERS STATIC ASSETS (and not the astro recipe's container path)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `templates/recipes/astro` is the *full-stack* recipe — Astro 5 SSR + Vue
// islands + Drizzle, deployed as a Docker image to Cloudflare Containers
// (see the recipe's `docker/dockerfiles/app.prod.Dockerfile`). This site is not
// that: `astro build` emits a fully static bundle (110 prerendered pages, zero
// server routes — see astro.config.mjs), so a container would be a running
// process whose only job is `sendfile`.
//
// So we take Cloudflare's current recommendation for a static bundle: a
// Workers **static assets** deployment. There is no `main` worker script here —
// an assets-only Worker serves `./dist` straight off Cloudflare's edge. This is
// also the successor to Cloudflare Pages for new projects, which is why we did
// not reach for `wrangler pages deploy`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RESPONSE HEADERS  (public/_headers)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `_headers` is NOT Pages-only. An earlier revision of this comment said it was
// — that was wrong. Workers static assets support it: `wrangler deploy` uploads
// `dist/_headers` as part of the assets manifest, and Cloudflare's asset worker
// applies the rules to every asset response (`wrangler dev` runs that same
// worker under workerd locally). `public/_headers` is where the immutable cache
// on `/_astro/*`, the site-wide HSTS/nosniff/referrer-policy, and the explicit
// text/plain on the llms artefacts live; read it for the syntax and its limits.
//
// Cloudflare's asset server also derives `Content-Type` from the file extension
// on its own, so `.txt` would render inline even with no `_headers` at all; the
// rules for `/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` pin that rather than rely on it.
// `.github/workflows/site.yml` re-asserts the whole set against a local workerd
// via `wrangler dev` before anything is deployed.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PREREQUISITES (nothing below works until these exist — see the workflow header)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1. mechcrate.dev registered and added as a zone in the Cloudflare account.
//   2. A Cloudflare API token with Workers Scripts:Edit + Workers Routes:Edit.
//   3. Repo secrets CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN and CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID.
// Until then `site.yml`'s deploy job skips cleanly; it never hard-fails.
{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/wrangler.json",
  "name": "mechcrate-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",

  "assets": {
    // Relative to this file (site/apps/site), i.e. the `astro build` output.
    "directory": "./dist",
    // Astro emits directory-style routes (`/docs/start/index.html`), so a
    // request for `/docs/start` must resolve to `/docs/start/`.
    "html_handling": "auto-trailing-slash",
    // Serve Astro's own `dist/404.html` (with site chrome) for unknown paths
    // instead of Cloudflare's bare 404.
    "not_found_handling": "404-page"
  },

  // Attaching the apex domain on deploy keeps the hostname declarative rather
  // than a dashboard click nobody remembers. Requires prerequisite (1) above:
  // if the zone is not in the account yet, `wrangler deploy` fails loudly with
  // "Could not find zone for mechcrate.dev" — which is the correct signal, not
  // a silent half-deploy.
  "routes": [
    {
      "pattern": "mechcrate.dev",
      "custom_domain": true
    }
  ]
}
